Harold is 75 years old and was a very successful entrepeneur during his career, most 
famously introducing mountain dew to Austria. his wife died six weeks ago and since then 
he has been 
having trouble taking care of himself and has been experiencing soul crushing loneliness. 
His children want him to move into a retirement home where simple meals will be cooked 
for him, he would have the company of other people and there will be someone there to 
contact emergency services if something were to happen to him. Karl wants to maintain his 
independence and he feels connected to both his apartment with the quiet little garden 
on the balcony and his ability to determine his life himself. He has also grown closer 
to a neighbour since his wife's passing and although his children think that this 
neighbour is a bad influence on him, they accept that he needs to blow off a bit of 
steam occasionally, but they believe that more time with this neighbour will require
someone checking in on him more often to ensure that the is ok. He specifically needs 
help organising the shopping, occasionally cooking simple, microwaveable meals and 
someone to call for help if he were to slip in the shower or be attacked by the 
gang who frequently congregate and loiter just outside his building.

Karl is 39 years old and his mother has been asking him about considering her moving 
in with him. Karl does not like that idea because he is a private person, a spontaneous 
soul, without a rigid sleeping schedule and also has some hobbies that he knows his 
mother will not approve of. Although it is mostly social interactions that Karl's 
mother is looking for, she is ironically a very socially anxious person and could 
not imagine herself joining a club or some other activity that would allow her 
to meet new people. Karl's mother is highly allegic to all pets, but a helping hand 
would definitely be fully sufficient for her needs and it would also help her to take 
care of herself as she ages. Luckily Karl's habits generate him a lot of money and 
a one-time expense of a helping hand would be within his budget.

Dr. Lise Korb is a care centre administrator in charge of one rehabilitation centre 
and two elderly care home. Dr Korb struggles to ensure all facilities are fully 
staffed over holidays and winter and she also finds that on an average day the care 
centres spend too much money on staff between the three centres, but that one 
of the three will generally not have enough employees. Legally Dr Korb cannot ask 
employees from one centre to work in another, but the tasks would be the same for 
each centre, helping prepare simple microwaveable meals, help patients in the shower, 
listen to patients and inform emergencies services if anything happens to one of the 
patients. One helping hand would be able to fill in at any of the facilities without 
notice, as well as watch over all of the patients and help with meal times.
